This page concludes a heuristic from the previous pages. There, Grothendieck computed H^2(S^2, A) ≅ A by gluing gerbes, treating S^2 as two discs glued along a circle. He took this as confirmation of a general principle: there is a theory of (homotopy) colimits of homotopy types, and the homotopy type of a 'glued' topos (a Lim of topoi) is the Lim (homotopy colimit) of the homotopy types of the pieces.

On this page he states the application he has in mind. Consider stratified spaces, in a sense to be made precise in §2, whose pieces (strata) are K(π,1)'s. The model examples are:
- the Teichmüller modular spaces M_{g,n};
- their compactifications (Deligne–Mumford–Knudsen M̄_{g,n}, stratified by boundary strata, each a product/quotient of smaller M_{g',n'}, which are K(π,1) orbifolds with mapping-class-group π);
- their subspaces;
- the corresponding tubular neighbourhoods.

For such objects, whatever their precise nature (orbifold/stack/topos), the canonical stratification should allow one to express the homotopy type as the homotopy colimit of a suitable inductive system, usually indexed by a finite set, of fundamental groupoids of the strata.

He then reflects that this 'integration' theory of homotopy types is relatively trivial and amenable to standard simplicial techniques. He had nebulously linked it to the 'grand yoga' relating relative homotopy structures over a topos to n-groupoids, the programme of Pursuing Stacks. He promises to return to it.

The next page (§2, 'Stratifications globales : formalisme simplicial (sans tubes)') starts exactly this simplicial formalism. Take a locally finite family of closed subspaces X_i indexed by a poset I, form X_{Δ0} = ⊔ X_i, and build the associated simplicial object; this is essentially the Čech/Segal-type nerve of a closed cover indexed by a poset.

The problem served is to compute homotopy types (and π_1, cohomology) of stratified moduli spaces, especially the Teichmüller tower and M̄_{g,n}, from mapping-class groupoids of the strata. This is the 'Lego-Teichmüller' philosophy of Esquisse d'un programme.
